,title,link,text,language,family
0,Siuslaw language - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Siuslaw_language,"January 1, 2026 - Siuslaw is currently considered to be a language isolate. It may be part of a Coast Oregon Penutian family together with Alsea and the Coosan languages, although the validity of this family is still controversial.",Siuslaw,Language isolate
1,Pacific Northwest languages - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pacific_Northwest_languages,"April 19, 2026 - The classification of Pacific Northwest ... area, between seven and eighteen different language families are represented in the region, including several language isolates and more than 50 member languages....",Siuslaw,Language isolate
2,Siuslaw people - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Siuslaw_people,"February 12, 2025 - The Siuslaw are closely related to the Lower Umpqua (or Kuitsh), and both group spoke dialects of Siuslaw language, a Coast Oregon Penutian language.",Siuslaw,Language isolate
3,"Siuslaw Language (SIS) – L1 & L2 Speakers, Status, Map, Endangered Level & Official Use | Ethnologue Free",https://www.ethnologue.com/language/sis/,Siuslaw is a dormant language of the United States. It is an isolate that is not known to be related to any other language. The language is no longer used as a first language by any remaining members of the ethnic community.,Siuslaw,Language isolate
4,Siuslaw language facts for kids,https://kids.kiddle.co/Siuslaw_language,"Siuslaw was a language spoken by the Siuslaw people and Lower Umpqua people in Oregon, United States. It is also known as Lower Umpqua. The Siuslaw language had two main types, or dialects: Siuslaw proper (called Šaayušƛa) and Lower Umpqua (called Qúuiič).",Siuslaw,Language isolate
5,Siuslaw language wiki | TheReaderWiki,https://thereaderwiki.com/en/Siuslaw_language,Siuslaw /saɪˈjuːslɔː/[3] was the language of the Siuslaw people and Lower Umpqua (Kuitsh) people of Oregon. It is also known as Lower Umpqua[a]. The Siuslaw language had two dialects: Siuslaw proper (Šaayušƛa) and Lower Umpqua (Quuiič).[4] Siuslaw is currently considered to be a language isolate.[5],Siuslaw,Language isolate
6,Languages - Siuslaw and Kuitsh: Native Americans of the Oregon Coast - LCC Research Guides at Lane Community College,https://libraryguides.lanecc.edu/c.php?g=598770&p=4145127,"December 2, 2025 - Milhau, John J. Vocabulary of the Dialects of Coos Bay (Anastich and Coos Bay). Letter to George Gibbs With Short Vocabularies of the Alsea, Siuslaw, Hanis and Miluk Languages. 1856. 12p. * Manuscript No. 191a in the National Anthropological Archives, Washington DC.",Siuslaw,Language isolate
7,Languages - Indigenous History of Oregon - LibGuides at Pacific University,https://pacificu.libguides.com/c.php?g=1050460&p=7789896,"February 27, 2024 - Alsea / Yaquina languages: Spoken on the Alsea and Yaquina rivers · Siuslaw / Kuitsh (or Kalawatset) languages: Siuslaw was spoken on the Siuslaw River, while Kuitsh was spoken on the lower Umpqua river near Winchester Bay.",Siuslaw,Language isolate
8,Endangered Languages Project - Siuslaw,https://www.endangeredlanguages.com/lang/8390,"Information from: “Ethnologue: Languages of the World, 19th Edition (2016)” . Lewis, M. Paul, Gary F. Simons, and Charles D. Fennig · SIL International · Native speakers worldwide · Ethnic population · 100 · MORE ON SPEAKER NUMBERS · ""No speakers by the 1970s"" MORE ON VITALITY · ""Confederated Tribes of Coos, Lower Umpqua and Siuslaw have a language program.""",Siuslaw,Language isolate
